    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 032325\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n425 PM MST Sat Oct 3 2026\n\n.UPDATE...\nUpdated Aviation\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Unseasonably strong high pressure will boost temperatures around 3 \nto 5 degrees above normal through the weekend, eventually reaching \ninto a well above normal range by the middle of next week as lower \ndesert highs reach 98-105 degrees.\n\n- Periods of breezy to locally windy conditions can be expected \nacross south-central and southwestern Arizona through Monday with \ngusts reaching 20-30 mph.\n\n- Dry weather with zero rain chances will persist into much of next \nweek.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nThe upper atmosphere remains mostly unchanged with an area of strong \nhigh pressure remaining dominant over the Desert SW, centered over N \nAZ, while low pressure remains to the regions east. The strong high \npressure is expected to peak this weekend with H5 heights reaching \nbetween 590-591 dam, which is near the 97th percent of climatology \nfor this time of year. With this unseasonably strong high \npressure, temperatures are expected to keep trending 3-5 degrees \nabove normal throughout the weekend. Afternoon highs across the \nwestern lower deserts and along the Colorado River valley will be \nthe upper 90s to low 100s. Meanwhile, south-central AZ will be \nslightly \"cooler\" with temperatures mostly staying below the century \nmark, however very localized areas may still see those dreaded \ntriple digits. Overnight lows will also remain well above normal (8F-\n12F), flirting with and likely breaking record warm lows throughout \nthe weekend and into next week. Expect lows across the region to be \nin the upper 70s to low 80s, and with the combination of above \nnormal highs and lows, moderate HeatRisk across the western deserts \nhas started to develop. \n\nWith the aforementioned pattern remaining in place dry northeasterly \nwinds aloft continue due to the increased pressure gradient. Winds \nassociated with this pressure gradient will continue to produce \ngusts reaching 20-30 mph at times across southcentral and southwest \nAZ. Periods of gusts between 35-40 mph could develop Sunday morning \nin the higher terrain of E And SE AZ as well as in southwestern AZ \nas winds become channeled through terrain features, before quickly \nrelaxing going into the afternoon hours. For the western portions, \npatches of blowing dust will be possible. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/... \nHeading into next week, deterministic and ensemble guidance show the \nunseasonably strong ridge weaken and retrograde wwd as an upper-lvl\ntrough progresses through the Intermountain West. This will allow \ntemperatures to dip a few degrees on Monday afternoon, although \nhighs will still remain slightly above normal. The aforementioned \ntrough will provide a temporary weakness in 500 mb hghts over \nsouthcentral AZ early next week, however positive hght anomalies \nwill still exist over SE CA and SW AZ which will maintain highs in \nthe low 100s. As ridging aloft begins to expand back over N AZ by \nmid-week, significant warming is progged to arrive with highs across \nthe western deserts topping out around 100-105 degrees and \napproaching 100 degrees for the central AZ deserts on Wednesday and \nThursday. These will likely be the hottest days of the forecast \nperiod before another trough potentially arrives onto the west coast \nby Friday into next weekend. Overall, subsidence aloft coupled with \nN-NE flow in the mid-lvls will maintain a dry forecast with near \nzero rain chances each day next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 2325Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nGusty east winds will be the primary weather issue through Sunday \nevening under clear skies. Confidence is very good that winds will \npartially decouple overnight, losing gustiness but maintaining \nmodest speeds. This should keep the majority of the region under \nLLWS criteria. Gusts will rapidly increase mid/late Sunday morning \nwith a period of 25-35kt gusts possible before gradually weakening \nthrough the afternoon. Gusts may be maintained longer into Sunday \nevening than is typical before weakening. Depending on wind gust \nstrength and recent land use, lofted dust affecting slantwise \nvisibility is possible Sunday afternoon/evening.\n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo major weather issues will exist through Sunday evening under \nclear skies. Confidence is excellent that a E/NE wind component will \nbe favored with a light north drift or variable directions around \nsunrise. A few gusts near 20kt will be possible Sunday afternoon. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDry conditions with slightly above normal temperatures are \nanticipated through this weekend before further warming occurs into \nthe middle of next week. MinRHs will mostly range from 15-25% over \nthe western districts to 25-35% for the eastern districts with fair \nto good overnight recovery area-wide. A persistent breezy easterly \ngradient wind will continue through at least Monday. Expect winds to \nremain elevated around 8-12 mph with early morning and afternoon \ngusts up to 20-30 mph. \n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan\nLONG TERM...Salerno \nAVIATION...18\nFIRE WEATHER...Salerno\n",
